  "commentary_text": "For sake of comparison, we have designed a single-stage, two-stage and a three-stage opamp for the same specifications. It is obvious that the single-stage opamp is the champion in power consumption. The addition of compensation capacitances invariably leads to excessive power consumption. However, a single-stage opamp does not provide a lot of gain. The addition of cascodes and gain boosting reduces the output swing. This is where a two-stage Miller opamp offers considerable advantages. A three-stage amplifier will be used whenever we need a class-AB output stage. Also when the supply voltage is so low that there is no room for cascodes, we may have to go for cascading rather than cascoding!",
